## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross-account access, least privilege, and auditability via STS, bucket policies, and logging. It also checks ability to minimize blast radius and plan tests.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account access with STS AssumeRole
- Least-privilege S3 object-level permission
- Bucket policy vs IAM policy boundary
- Session duration and revocation
- CloudTrail and S3 data events for auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<VENDOR_ID>:root" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": { "Bool": { "aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "true" } }
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::company-reports-prod/2025/Q4/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<VENDOR_ID>:role/VendorReadOnly" },
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::company-reports-prod/2025/Q4/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you enforce revocation if the vendor's role is compromised?
- How would you test that access is strictly limited to the 2025/Q4 prefix?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement tenant isolation with least privilege, plus rapid containment using AWS-native controls. It also covers S3 prefix scoping, per-tenant KMS keys, CloudTrail data events, and automated revocation.

## Key Concepts
- Least privilege IAM for Lambda and prefix-scoped S3 access
- S3 bucket policies with bucket-level and object-prefix constraints
- Per-tenant KMS keys and rotation
- CloudTrail Data Events for S3 access
- EventBridge-based containment workflow (60s)

## Code Example
```javascript
// IAM policy snippet for Lambda role
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake",
        "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/tenant-id-${aws:PrincipalTag tenantId}/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringLike": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-id-*"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle role cred leakage or bucket misconfiguration?
- How would you test the containment workflow end-to-end?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design a secure, cross-account multi-tenant streaming architecture with strict tenant isolation, fine-grained access controls, key management best practices, and rapid incident response capabilities in a production data pipeline context.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account trust relationships with ABAC and MFA enforcement
- Tenant isolation across streaming data paths using account boundaries
- Per-tenant KMS keys with automatic rotation for cryptographic isolation
- PrivateLink/VPC endpoints for private, encrypted data transit without internet exposure
- Immutable audit trails using CloudTrail, S3 Object Lock, and Lake Formation access controls
- Automated breach containment workflow with sub-minute response times

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": ["kinesis:PutRecord", "kinesis:PutRecords"],
    "Resource": "arn:aws:kinesis:*:*:stream/tenant-${aws:PrincipalTag/TenantId}/*",
    "Condition": {
      "StringEquals": {
        "aws:SourceAccount": "${aws:PrincipalTag/TenantId}",
        "aws:RequestedRegion": ["us-east-1", "us-west-2"]
      },
      "ArnEquals": {
        "aws:SourceArn": "arn:aws:kinesis:*:*:stream/tenant-${aws:PrincipalTag/TenantId}/*"
      },
      "Bool": {"aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent": "true"}
    }
  }]
}
```
